  <!-- LSAT improvement guide -->
  <section id="lsat-improve" class="section-pad scroll-mt-24 border-t border-[var(--line)]">
    <div class="page-shell">
      <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-navy-950">
        How to Improve Your LSAT Score
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
        Retaking the LSAT is the single highest-leverage move most applicants can make, but the
        right plan depends on where you are starting. Here is a playbook for each starting band,
        based on how much a score jump actually changes your school options.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Starting below 150</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-navy-800">Target: 155-160</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Below 150, the biggest wins come from fundamentals, not advanced technique. Master the
            three section types — logical reasoning, reading comprehension, and analytical
            reasoning — with a structured course or tutor, and drill untimed before adding pacing.
            A jump from 145 to 158 is very achievable for most people and completely changes your
            school options: it moves you from the least selective schools into the Top 60-100 band
            and unlocks scholarships. Plan for 3-6 months of consistent study.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Starting at 150-159</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-navy-800">Target: 163-168</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            This is where the highest-return gains live. The jump from 158 to 165 moves you from
            the 70th to the 93rd percentile and opens the Top 25-50 schools. Focus on identifying
            your weakest section and drilling it relentlessly, reviewing every wrong answer to
            understand the reasoning pattern, and building test-day pacing. A 5-7 point gain is
            realistic with 2-4 months of focused study, and it is the cheapest way to upgrade your
            entire school list.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Starting at 160-169</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-navy-800">Target: 170-174</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Above 160, gains come from eliminating the last few mistakes, not learning new
            material. The jump from 166 to 171 clears the median at most T14 schools and is often
            the difference between a reach and a target. Drill the hardest question types, take
            timed, full-length practice tests weekly, and analyze every miss in writing. A 3-5
            point gain is realistic for disciplined studiers and is the single highest-leverage
            move for T14 applicants sitting just below medians.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--soft)] p-6">
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
          <span class="font-semibold text-navy-900">The rule of thumb: </span>
          if you are scoring meaningfully higher on recent, timed practice tests than your last
          real score, a retake is almost always worth it — the return on a few extra LSAT points
          is larger than the return on anything else in your application. Use the retake-impact
          tool above to see exactly how much a gain would change your odds at your target schools.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

