  <section id="soft-factors" class="section-pad scroll-mt-24 border-t border-[var(--line)]">
    <div class="page-shell">
      <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-navy-950">
        Beyond the Numbers: What Else Moves Your Chances
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
        Your LSAT and GPA decide which schools are realistic, but the soft factors — background,
        experience, essays, recommendations, and timing — often decide whether you actually get in
        at the schools where your numbers are competitive. Here is what each one is worth.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Underrepresented minority (URM) status</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Law schools place real, measurable value on diversity, and applicants from
            underrepresented racial and ethnic groups often see a meaningful boost at the same
            LSAT and GPA — in practice, a URM applicant can be competitive at schools where their
            numbers sit several points below the median. The effect varies by school and year, and
            admissions policies around race have been shifting, so treat it as a genuine but
            unpredictable advantage rather than a guarantee.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Work experience</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Work experience is growing in importance, and a majority of students at schools like
            Northwestern arrive with one to three years of it. It gives you maturity, concrete
            material for essays, and a clearer answer to "why law." It can partly offset a softer
            GPA, but it cannot compensate for an LSAT far below a school's median. If your numbers
            are average, a year or two of meaningful work is one of the best investments you can make.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Personal statement and essays</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Essays matter most at the margins and at the very top. With identical numbers, a strong
            personal statement can tip a close decision, while a weak one can sink strong stats. At
            Yale, Stanford, and Harvard — where nearly every applicant has elite numbers — the
            essays are often what separate admits from waitlists. A compelling narrative about a
            genuine commitment beats a list of achievements every time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Letters of recommendation</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Recommendations carry real weight, and a detailed, enthusiastic letter from a professor
            who knows your work well beats a generic letter from a famous name. A lukewarm or vague
            letter is a genuine negative, so choose recommenders who can write specifically about
            your analytical ability and writing. Two academic recommenders plus one professional
            (if you have worked) is the right mix for most applicants.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Early decision</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Early decision can provide a measurable boost at schools that offer it — Duke, Cornell,
            and Georgetown are known to give ED a real edge — because it signals strong interest and
            lets the school lock in a committed applicant. Yale, Stanford, and Harvard do not offer
            binding ED. The catch: ED is usually binding, so only apply ED to a school you would
            attend regardless of cost, and understand you may lose scholarship negotiating leverage.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Applying early in the cycle</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Because most schools use rolling admissions, submitting by November is one of the few
            factors entirely within your control that measurably improves your odds. Seats and
            scholarship budgets are fuller later in the cycle, and the effect is largest for
            borderline applicants sitting near a school's medians. Treat the October-November
            window as a real strategic advantage, not a nice-to-have.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

