    <div class="page-shell">
      <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-navy-950">
        Outcomes, Debt, and Return on Investment
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-[var(--muted)]">
        Getting in is only half the decision. Before you commit three years and tens of thousands
        of dollars, understand what each school actually delivers — employment, bar passage, and
        the debt you will carry.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Employment outcomes</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            The return on a law degree depends heavily on whether graduates actually become
            lawyers. The ABA publishes employment data for every accredited school, and the gaps
            are enormous: some schools place over 90% of graduates in full-time, long-term,
            bar-passage-required jobs, while others place under 50%. Check the "bar passage
            required" employment rate specifically — it is the single most honest measure of a
            school's ability to make you a lawyer.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Bar passage rates</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Bar passage is the gatekeeper to a legal career, and rates vary widely by school. A
            school ranked 20 spots lower with a 95% first-time bar passage rate can be a far better
            investment than a higher-ranked school with a 70% rate. Always check both first-time
            and ultimate bar passage, and be wary of schools where a large share of graduates
            cannot pass the bar on their first attempt.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">The cost of law school</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Annual tuition at T14 schools runs roughly $63,000-$82,000, and total cost of
            attendance often exceeds $100,000 per year at private schools. Many graduates carry
            $150,000-$200,000 in debt. Whether that debt is manageable depends on your career path:
            big-law associates earn $200,000+ in their first year, while public-interest and
            smaller-firm lawyers earn far less and often rely on loan forgiveness programs.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-white p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">Scholarships and aid</h3>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
            Scholarships track the same medians as admission: the more you are above a school's
            LSAT and GPA medians, the larger the offer. An applicant at the 75th percentile of both
            can often secure substantial aid — sometimes full tuition — while someone at the 25th
            percentile may receive little or none. If minimizing debt matters to you, target schools
            where your numbers are above the medians rather than chasing the highest rank.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--soft)] p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-navy-900">The honest rule</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-[var(--muted)]">
          Attend the best school you can afford without excessive debt, and match your school
          choice to your actual career goals rather than prestige alone. A full-ride at a strong
          regional school can be a smarter financial decision than sticker price at a T14 — the
          math depends on what you want to do and where you want to practice. Use the cost
          calculator above to estimate your application budget, then research each school's
          employment and bar passage data before you decide.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

